1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,667
After an incident, three sentences from
a CTO, straight from the first page of

2
00:00:09,707 --> 00:00:10,401
Chapter 18.

3
00:00:10,741 --> 00:00:13,829
"main is protected. How did
a force push get through?"

4
00:00:14,169 --> 00:00:17,646
"The pull request has two approvals
and every check is green.

5
00:00:17,686 --> 00:00:19,376
Why is the merge button grey?"

6
00:00:19,716 --> 00:00:23,121
"Who is allowed to skip our rules,
and would we know if they did?"

7
00:00:23,461 --> 00:00:28,366
All three have precise answers, and none
of them requires guessing what GitHub did.

8
00:00:28,406 --> 00:00:33,311
A rule is a small, mechanical thing: a
condition checked on the server before a ref

9
00:00:33,351 --> 00:00:37,064
moves. A ref is a name for a
commit, such as a branch or a tag.

10
00:00:37,104 --> 00:00:42,068
Once you see a rule that way, "how did it
get through" becomes a list of four things

11
00:00:42,108 --> 00:00:45,106
to check, in order. Hold
on to the three questions.

12
00:00:45,146 --> 00:00:47,489
Each one gets its answer before the end.

13
00:00:49,329 --> 00:00:53,009
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:53,049 --> 00:00:57,888
The pull request chapter ended with a list
of controls that are only advisory: a

15
00:00:57,928 --> 00:01:01,486
"changes requested" review, a
red check, a CODEOWNERS file.

16
00:01:01,526 --> 00:01:04,840
This module is about the layer
that makes them binding.

17
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:10,390
On GitHub in 2026 that layer has two
mechanisms that coexist: rulesets and classic

18
00:01:10,430 --> 00:01:15,768
branch protection rules. Most "why can't I
merge" tickets come from not knowing that

19
00:01:15,808 --> 00:01:21,082
both are evaluated. The chapter teaches
rulesets first, because GitHub now says so:

20
00:01:21,122 --> 00:01:24,924
"Rulesets are the recommended
way to protect your branches".

21
00:01:25,264 --> 00:01:30,372
This video covers what a rule is, what a
ruleset is, how several of them combine,

22
00:01:30,412 --> 00:01:35,394
and who can skip them. This is GitHub,
not Git: the rules live on the platform.

23
00:01:35,734 --> 00:01:41,587
But the mechanism can be modelled in
plain Git, and that is the demonstration:

24
00:01:41,627 --> 00:01:47,783
labs/ch18/ref-updates.sh. Inside the lab,
a bare repository plays the server and a

25
00:01:47,823 --> 00:01:54,054
hook written for the course imitates five
rules. It is not GitHub's implementation.

26
00:01:54,094 --> 00:01:59,192
The second replay, labs/ch18/fnmatch-targets.sh,
runs the documented

27
00:01:59,232 --> 00:02:03,574
pattern-matching function. Then
a walkthrough of Lab 23.1.

28
00:02:03,914 --> 00:02:09,492
Labels. git push --force to a shared branch
is 🔴 DANGEROUS: it removes commits from

29
00:02:09,532 --> 00:02:15,110
the remote branch. Preview with git log
between your branch and the remote-tracking

30
00:02:15,150 --> 00:02:20,728
branch in both directions. In the replay
it is aimed at a sandbox server, mostly to

31
00:02:20,768 --> 00:02:26,414
be refused. On the GitHub side, the lab
creates a ruleset with gh api --method POST,

32
00:02:26,454 --> 00:02:32,032
which the chapter's table labels 🔴, for a
reason I will read out when we get there.

33
00:02:32,072 --> 00:02:35,281
gh ruleset list and gh
ruleset check are 🟢 SAFE.

34
00:02:37,121 --> 00:02:38,533
After this video you can:

35
00:02:38,873 --> 00:02:44,207
Describe a rule as a check on a ref update
and say which rules need only the old ID,

36
00:02:44,247 --> 00:02:45,998
the new ID and the ref name.

37
00:02:46,338 --> 00:02:50,946
Reproduce force-push, deletion and merge-commit
rules on a local server with a hook.

38
00:02:51,286 --> 00:02:55,524
Say what a ruleset targets and what
its three enforcement statuses do.

39
00:02:55,864 --> 00:03:00,130
Compute the effective requirement when
several rulesets and a classic rule apply.

40
00:03:00,470 --> 00:03:04,097
Distinguish bypass from exemption
and say what trace each leaves.

41
00:03:05,937 --> 00:03:11,098
In one sentence: a rule is a condition that
GitHub evaluates on its server before it

42
00:03:11,138 --> 00:03:15,991
lets a ref move, and neither your client
nor your --force flag has a say in it.

43
00:03:16,331 --> 00:03:22,037
Precisely. Every change to a repository on
a server is a ref update: a ref name, the

44
00:03:22,077 --> 00:03:25,868
old object ID, the new one.
A push proposes ref updates.

45
00:03:25,908 --> 00:03:31,615
A merge button is GitHub performing one. In
the remotes videos you saw the plain-Git

46
00:03:31,655 --> 00:03:37,157
hook that sees each proposal first,
pre-receive: a program the server runs before

47
00:03:37,197 --> 00:03:38,114
any ref moves.

48
00:03:38,454 --> 00:03:41,778
Five ruleset rules are predicates
on those three values.

49
00:03:42,118 --> 00:03:47,812
Restrict creations: is the old ID all zeros?
Restrict deletions: is the new ID all

50
00:03:47,852 --> 00:03:51,798
zeros? Block force pushes: is
old not an ancestor of new?

51
00:03:51,838 --> 00:03:57,393
Require linear history: does the range
from old to new contain a commit with two

52
00:03:57,433 --> 00:04:02,917
parents? Restrict updates: are both IDs
non-zero, that is, does an existing ref

53
00:04:02,957 --> 00:04:03,267
move?

54
00:04:03,607 --> 00:04:08,820
The remaining rules, a pull request, approvals,
status checks, signatures, ask about

55
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:14,072
GitHub objects attached to the new commits.
They need the platform's database, which

56
00:04:14,112 --> 00:04:16,386
is why plain Git cannot imitate them.

57
00:04:16,726 --> 00:04:22,337
Rulesets. In one sentence: a ruleset is
a named list of rules with three more

58
00:04:22,377 --> 00:04:26,960
things. A target: which refs. An
enforcement status: on or off.

59
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,977
And a bypass list: who is excepted. You
can have up to 75 rulesets per repository,

60
00:04:33,017 --> 00:04:35,473
and 75 organization-wide rulesets.

61
00:04:35,813 --> 00:04:40,959
There are three kinds. A branch ruleset
targets branches by name pattern, or "the

62
00:04:40,999 --> 00:04:44,801
default branch". A tag ruleset
targets tags by name pattern.

63
00:04:44,841 --> 00:04:49,923
A push ruleset applies to every push to
the repository and its fork network, and

64
00:04:49,963 --> 00:04:55,174
blocks file paths, extensions and sizes.
The last two are the subject of the video

65
00:04:55,214 --> 00:04:55,878
after next.

66
00:04:56,218 --> 00:05:01,565
Target patterns use fnmatch syntax, and
GitHub names the exact function and flag.

67
00:05:01,605 --> 00:05:07,151
The consequence, in its words: "the * wildcard
does not match directory separators".

68
00:05:07,491 --> 00:05:12,063
Try it now, thirty seconds, on
paper. The pattern is qa/*.

69
00:05:12,103 --> 00:05:17,310
Does it cover a branch named
qa/foo/bar? Write yes or no, and why.

70
00:05:17,350 --> 00:05:18,106
I'll wait.

71
00:05:22,946 --> 00:05:29,012
No. qa/* matches branches beginning with
qa/ and containing a single slash, and does

72
00:05:29,052 --> 00:05:34,537
not match qa/foo/bar. A pattern that
silently fails to match leaves a branch

73
00:05:34,577 --> 00:05:40,426
unprotected, with nothing to tell you. In
the REST API two special targets exist,

74
00:05:40,466 --> 00:05:46,314
~DEFAULT_BRANCH and ~ALL. Prefer the first
for main: it follows a renamed default

75
00:05:46,354 --> 00:05:46,823
branch.

76
00:05:47,163 --> 00:05:52,093
Enforcement status. The documentation for
Free, Pro and Team lists two: Active,

77
00:05:52,133 --> 00:05:55,868
"your ruleset will be enforced
upon creation", and Disabled.

78
00:05:55,908 --> 00:06:01,026
The Enterprise Cloud documentation adds
Evaluate: the ruleset is not enforced, but

79
00:06:01,066 --> 00:06:06,185
you can monitor on the Rule Insights page
which actions would or would not violate

80
00:06:06,225 --> 00:06:11,470
rules. One caveat marked unverified: whether
Evaluate mode is available to Team-plan

81
00:06:11,510 --> 00:06:13,735
organization rulesets is not stated.

82
00:06:14,075 --> 00:06:18,719
A disabled ruleset protects nothing and
still looks reassuring in a settings page.

83
00:06:18,759 --> 00:06:23,231
Disabling is how rulesets get "temporarily"
switched off during an incident and

84
00:06:23,271 --> 00:06:23,803
forgotten.

85
00:06:24,143 --> 00:06:27,090
Layering. In one sentence:
rulesets have no priority order.

86
00:06:27,130 --> 00:06:31,141
All active rulesets that target a ref,
and the classic rule that matches it, are

87
00:06:31,181 --> 00:06:34,230
added together, and for each
rule the strictest version wins.

88
00:06:34,270 --> 00:06:37,166
The documentation: "A ruleset
does not have a priority ...

89
00:06:37,206 --> 00:06:39,749
the rules in each of these
rulesets are aggregated.

90
00:06:39,789 --> 00:06:43,850
If the same rule is defined in different
ways across the aggregated rulesets, the

91
00:06:43,890 --> 00:06:46,179
most restrictive version
of the rule applies."

92
00:06:46,519 --> 00:06:50,754
Two consequences. You cannot loosen
a branch by editing one layer.

93
00:06:50,794 --> 00:06:55,871
And a repository ruleset can add to an
organization ruleset and never subtract.

94
00:06:55,911 --> 00:07:00,988
One more fact: forks do not inherit branch
or tag rulesets from their upstream.

95
00:07:01,028 --> 00:07:02,931
They do inherit push rulesets.

96
00:07:03,271 --> 00:07:08,453
Bypass. In one sentence: a ruleset applies
to everyone, administrators included,

97
00:07:08,493 --> 00:07:10,868
except the actors on its bypass list.

98
00:07:11,208 --> 00:07:16,795
Eligible for the list are repository
admins, organization owners and enterprise

99
00:07:16,835 --> 00:07:21,496
owners. The maintain or write role.
Teams, excluding secret teams.

100
00:07:21,536 --> 00:07:27,052
GitHub Apps. Dependabot. And, since the
seventh of May 2026, individual users.

101
00:07:27,092 --> 00:07:28,619
Each entry has a mode.

102
00:07:28,959 --> 00:07:33,651
always: the actor may push directly and
merge despite the rules, and a bypass is

103
00:07:33,691 --> 00:07:38,442
recorded. pull_request: the actor must
open a pull request, and may then merge it

104
00:07:38,482 --> 00:07:43,411
despite unmet rules. That leaves, in GitHub's
words, "a clear trail of their changes

105
00:07:43,451 --> 00:07:45,481
in the pull request and audit log".

106
00:07:45,821 --> 00:07:51,268
exempt: the rules are not run for this
actor, and no trace is left: "a bypass audit

107
00:07:51,308 --> 00:07:56,754
entry will not be created". The exempt mode
arrived on the tenth of September 2025.

108
00:07:56,794 --> 00:08:02,306
GitHub's own contrast: a standard bypass is
"a 'break glass' action" that "generates

109
00:08:02,346 --> 00:08:07,329
prominent audit signals", whereas "an
exemption silently skips enforcement".

110
00:08:07,669 --> 00:08:13,463
Quick quiz. Which mode lets someone skip
the rules without your ever knowing?

111
00:08:13,503 --> 00:08:17,328
A: always. B: pull_request.
C: exempt. Your answer?

112
00:08:22,168 --> 00:08:27,127
C. And that answers the CTO's third question.
With an empty bypass list nobody can

113
00:08:27,167 --> 00:08:30,663
skip the rules, including the
repository's administrators.

114
00:08:30,703 --> 00:08:34,747
With pull_request bypass you would
know. With exempt you would not.

115
00:08:35,087 --> 00:08:39,997
And a point that surprises people who know
classic protection: the Maintain role's

116
00:08:40,037 --> 00:08:44,465
documented right to push to protected
branches does not apply to rulesets.

117
00:08:44,505 --> 00:08:49,113
Under rulesets, roles grant nothing by
themselves. Only the bypass list does.

118
00:08:50,953 --> 00:08:55,502
The textbook's analogy: a bank teller will
move money between accounts for anyone

119
00:08:55,542 --> 00:08:59,921
with the right card, but checks each
transfer against the account's conditions

120
00:08:59,961 --> 00:09:04,113
first: two signatures above a limit,
no withdrawals from a frozen account.

121
00:09:04,153 --> 00:09:07,739
The card is your write permission.
The conditions are the rules.

122
00:09:08,079 --> 00:09:12,516
The analogy breaks in one place: on GitHub
the conditions are published, and anyone

123
00:09:12,556 --> 00:09:14,782
who can read the repository can read them.

124
00:09:15,122 --> 00:09:19,634
Keep the two apart in every incident.
"She has write access" is about the card.

125
00:09:19,674 --> 00:09:24,475
"The push was refused" is about a condition.
And several sheets of conditions can be

126
00:09:24,515 --> 00:09:29,315
attached to the same account: the teller
applies all of them, and for each condition

127
00:09:29,355 --> 00:09:30,583
the strictest version.

128
00:09:32,423 --> 00:09:37,053
On the left, your push. It proposes
one thing: old ID, new ID, ref name.

129
00:09:37,093 --> 00:09:42,307
On the right, the server collects every
active ruleset that targets the ref, plus

130
00:09:42,347 --> 00:09:47,172
the classic rule that matches the branch.
If all rules pass, the ref moves.

131
00:09:47,212 --> 00:09:51,842
If one fails, the push is rejected,
and the words are "remote rejected".

132
00:09:52,182 --> 00:09:57,081
Four words in that picture are the four
checks for the CTO's first question, "how

133
00:09:57,121 --> 00:10:01,166
did a force push get through". Was
the ruleset active at that time?

134
00:10:01,206 --> 00:10:05,007
Did it target that branch name?
Was the actor on a bypass list?

135
00:10:05,047 --> 00:10:07,385
And was the rule in the ruleset at all?

136
00:10:07,725 --> 00:10:13,068
Four layers. Add up every rule, and where
one rule appears more than once, approvals

137
00:10:13,108 --> 00:10:18,194
at 1, 3 and 2, take the strictest: three.
That is the grey merge button with two

138
00:10:18,234 --> 00:10:22,424
approvals: some layer that nobody
was looking at asks for a third.

139
00:10:26,764 --> 00:10:32,299
The hook. Read the comment at the top:
standard input has one line per proposed ref

140
00:10:32,339 --> 00:10:37,739
update, old ID, new ID, ref name. Everything
the five rules need is on that line.

141
00:10:37,779 --> 00:10:41,097
The case statement is the
target: refs/heads/main.

142
00:10:50,237 --> 00:10:55,011
Installing it on the bare server and making
it executable is "creating a ruleset and

143
00:10:55,051 --> 00:10:58,563
setting it to Active". Here is
the branch main on that server.

144
00:11:03,103 --> 00:11:07,922
A fast-forward still goes through. No
rule asks anything a fast-forward fails.

145
00:11:08,262 --> 00:11:13,300
Now the force push. The five answers for
git push --force to a shared branch.

146
00:11:13,340 --> 00:11:18,773
It replaces the remote branch with yours.
It can destroy commits on the server that

147
00:11:18,813 --> 00:11:23,916
your branch does not contain. Preview
with git log between the remote-tracking

148
00:11:23,956 --> 00:11:29,390
branch and your branch, in both directions.
Recovery is the subject of the recovery

149
00:11:29,430 --> 00:11:33,346
chapter and needs the old ID
from someone's clone or reflog.

150
00:11:33,386 --> 00:11:37,237
And it is appropriate on a
shared branch essentially never.

151
00:11:37,277 --> 00:11:41,127
Here the target is a sandbox,
and the point is the refusal.

152
00:11:43,467 --> 00:11:47,351
The server blocks force pushes.
--force will be refused.

153
00:11:47,391 --> 00:11:49,453
What about --force-with-lease?

154
00:11:49,793 --> 00:11:54,968
Refused as well. Read the two rejections.
"Remote rejected" means the server said

155
00:11:55,008 --> 00:11:59,281
no, as opposed to plain "rejected",
which is your own Git refusing.

156
00:11:59,321 --> 00:12:04,689
--force-with-lease fared no better than
--force: both only switch off client checks.

157
00:12:05,029 --> 00:12:09,396
And on the server, main has not
moved. It is still at 810dc2f.

158
00:12:09,436 --> 00:12:13,803
Next, a deletion. Predict which
rule answers. Say it out loud.

159
00:12:18,643 --> 00:12:24,073
Restrict deletions. Deletion is a ref
update too: the new ID is all zeros.

160
00:12:26,413 --> 00:12:31,246
The linear-history rule is active. A merge
commit is pushed, then a squash commit.

161
00:12:31,286 --> 00:12:32,315
Which is accepted?

162
00:12:32,655 --> 00:12:37,610
The merge commit is refused. The squash
commit, an ordinary one-parent commit, is

163
00:12:37,650 --> 00:12:42,666
accepted. That is the whole content of
GitHub's sentence that under this rule pull

164
00:12:42,706 --> 00:12:45,933
requests "must use a squash
merge or a rebase merge".

165
00:12:45,973 --> 00:12:50,989
The git reset --hard in between discards
the local merge commit in a clean sandbox

166
00:12:51,029 --> 00:12:51,791
working tree.

167
00:12:52,131 --> 00:12:58,170
So main moves once more, to 8c7e96f. Every
accepted update is on this line, and the

168
00:12:58,210 --> 00:13:00,074
refused ones left no mark.

169
00:13:00,414 --> 00:13:05,653
The rules target main. Another branch,
scratch, may still be rewritten and deleted.

170
00:13:05,693 --> 00:13:09,278
A rule protects the refs its
target names, and no others.

171
00:13:09,618 --> 00:13:14,537
And enforcement status "disabled": the
executable bit is removed, and the same kind

172
00:13:14,577 --> 00:13:19,138
of force push goes through. Git even
prints a hint that the hook was ignored.

173
00:13:19,178 --> 00:13:22,484
GitHub's settings page prints
no such hint on your push.

174
00:13:30,124 --> 00:13:35,794
qa/* matches one level. qa/**/*
matches any number of slashes.

175
00:13:38,134 --> 00:13:42,061
*feature*. Does it protect
a branch called feature/x?

176
00:13:42,401 --> 00:13:47,787
It does not, although the documentation uses
that very pattern as an example of "any

177
00:13:47,827 --> 00:13:52,503
branches matching": the star stops at
the slash. release/* does not cover

178
00:13:52,543 --> 00:13:57,929
release/1.0/hotfix. And a star alone matches
no branch that has a slash in its name.

179
00:13:58,269 --> 00:14:03,474
The lab creates the ruleset from a JSON
file kept in the course, through the REST

180
00:14:03,514 --> 00:14:08,396
API. The chapter's safety table labels
that call 🔴, and gives the reason: it

181
00:14:08,436 --> 00:14:13,836
destroys nothing, but every gh api call that
is not a GET does whatever the endpoint

182
00:14:13,876 --> 00:14:16,686
and the JSON say, with
all your permissions.

183
00:14:17,026 --> 00:14:21,740
And if the ruleset is Active, it
binds everyone at once, you included.

184
00:14:21,780 --> 00:14:27,106
The preview is to create it with enforcement
disabled and run gh ruleset check.

185
00:14:27,146 --> 00:14:30,706
The recovery is to set
enforcement to disabled again.

186
00:14:30,746 --> 00:14:36,208
It is appropriate for rules kept as reviewed
JSON, which is what the lab file is.

187
00:14:36,248 --> 00:14:38,449
Read the file before you send it.

188
00:14:38,789 --> 00:14:43,510
gh ruleset check main asks GitHub
which rules apply to that branch name.

189
00:14:43,550 --> 00:14:46,883
With --web it opens the
same answer in the browser.

190
00:14:46,923 --> 00:14:51,777
In the repository's settings, the
rulesets page lists the ruleset with its

191
00:14:51,817 --> 00:14:55,149
enforcement status, its
target and its bypass list.

192
00:14:55,189 --> 00:14:58,125
Name what you see there
by those three words.

193
00:14:58,465 --> 00:15:00,138
Then test it as the replay did:

194
00:15:00,478 --> 00:15:04,820
Predict each answer before you run it,
including the words "remote rejected".

195
00:15:04,860 --> 00:15:09,260
The lab then goes the allowed way, through
a pull request, and tries two merge

196
00:15:09,300 --> 00:15:14,040
methods against the ruleset. Its local Part
A also shows layering with two plain-Git

197
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:18,536
layers: the hook is disabled and the force
push is still refused, by the server

198
00:15:18,576 --> 00:15:20,585
setting receive.denyNonFastForwards.

199
00:15:22,425 --> 00:15:24,073
Five mistakes to watch for.

200
00:15:24,413 --> 00:15:28,037
Believing --force-with-lease
can get past a server rule.

201
00:15:28,077 --> 00:15:33,141
Root cause: both force options only switch
off client-side checks; the rule is

202
00:15:33,181 --> 00:15:34,711
evaluated on the server.

203
00:15:35,051 --> 00:15:39,793
Targeting feature* or release/* and
assuming nested branch names are covered.

204
00:15:39,833 --> 00:15:44,948
Root cause: the star does not match a slash;
a pattern that fails to match protects

205
00:15:44,988 --> 00:15:46,501
nothing and says nothing.

206
00:15:46,841 --> 00:15:50,077
Loosening one ruleset and
finding the merge still blocked.

207
00:15:50,117 --> 00:15:54,652
Root cause: all active rulesets and the
classic rule are aggregated, and the most

208
00:15:54,692 --> 00:15:56,968
restrictive version of each rule applies.

209
00:15:57,307 --> 00:16:01,515
Assuming administrators or the
Maintain role can push past a ruleset.

210
00:16:01,555 --> 00:16:05,578
Root cause: under rulesets only
the bypass list grants exceptions.

211
00:16:05,918 --> 00:16:10,666
Leaving a ruleset disabled after an
emergency. Root cause: a disabled ruleset

212
00:16:10,706 --> 00:16:14,148
enforces nothing and still
appears in the settings page.

213
00:16:15,988 --> 00:16:19,097
Now, out of the lab. "How did
a force push get through?"

214
00:16:19,137 --> 00:16:22,640
An ML platform team answers it
in the order the textbook gives.

215
00:16:22,980 --> 00:16:27,809
Was the ruleset Active at that time? The
ruleset on main was set to Disabled three

216
00:16:27,849 --> 00:16:31,312
weeks earlier, during a migration,
and never switched back.

217
00:16:31,352 --> 00:16:35,291
That alone answers the question.
They check the other three anyway.

218
00:16:35,631 --> 00:16:40,687
Did it target that branch name? The
target was the default branch, so yes.

219
00:16:40,727 --> 00:16:46,404
Their second ruleset, for release branches,
used release/*, and the hotfix branches

220
00:16:46,444 --> 00:16:50,330
are named release/2.4/hotfix-1:
those were never covered.

221
00:16:50,670 --> 00:16:55,363
Was the actor on a bypass list?
One entry: a team, in always mode.

222
00:16:55,403 --> 00:16:58,231
Was the rule in the ruleset at all? Yes.

223
00:16:58,571 --> 00:17:03,592
Their changes follow section 18.5. The
bypass list becomes short and is made of

224
00:17:03,632 --> 00:17:08,972
roles or teams, not people. Humans get
pull_request mode, so that an emergency merge

225
00:17:09,012 --> 00:17:14,097
still leaves a pull request. always is
reserved for the one automation that must

226
00:17:14,137 --> 00:17:19,413
push, the release bot, under its own GitHub
App identity, so that the entry names a

227
00:17:19,453 --> 00:17:24,474
thing they can audit. Nobody is exempt.
And the ruleset JSON goes under version

228
00:17:24,514 --> 00:17:24,986
control.

229
