lab-manual/m23-governance.md. Part A runs
in the lab shell on a bare server, and

231
00:17:36,384 --> 00:17:41,699
Part B on GitHub. Before each push in
Part A, write the three values of the ref

232
00:17:41,739 --> 00:17:47,189
update, old, new and ref, and name the
rule that will refuse it, or say that none

233
00:17:47,229 --> 00:17:52,409
will. In the failure scenario, predict
what happens when one of two layers is

234
00:17:52,449 --> 00:17:53,290
switched off.

235
00:17:53,630 --> 00:18:01,155
The challenge is Exercise 23.4, "Layers",
in exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

236
00:18:02,995 --> 00:18:06,140
Question 221 of the CTO question bank:

237
00:18:08,480 --> 00:18:13,122
Answer out loud. A strong answer defines
a ref update and where it is evaluated.

238
00:18:13,162 --> 00:18:17,803
It lists the rules of the first kind with
the question each asks about the three

239
00:18:17,843 --> 00:18:22,134
values, and explains for the second
kind what the platform has to look up.

240
00:18:22,174 --> 00:18:26,991
It mentions what a force flag does and does
not change, and how you would model the

241
00:18:27,031 --> 00:18:28,512
first kind without GitHub.

242
00:18:30,352 --> 00:18:34,985
Let's land this. Three questions from
a CTO came in, and each one now has a

243
00:18:35,025 --> 00:18:36,106
mechanical answer.

244
00:18:36,446 --> 00:18:38,106
You should now be able to say:

245
00:18:38,446 --> 00:18:43,499
A rule is a server-side condition on a
ref update: old ID, new ID, ref name.

246
00:18:43,839 --> 00:18:48,645
Creation, deletion, force push, linear
history and update restrictions need only

247
00:18:48,685 --> 00:18:52,886
those three values; reviews, checks
and signatures need platform data.

248
00:18:53,226 --> 00:18:58,644
A ruleset has a target, an enforcement
status and a bypass list; * in a target does

249
00:18:58,684 --> 00:18:59,828
not cross a slash.

250
00:19:00,168 --> 00:19:04,697
All applicable rulesets and the classic
rule add up, and the strictest version of

251
00:19:04,737 --> 00:19:05,600
each rule holds.

252
00:19:05,940 --> 00:19:11,232
Bypass leaves a trace; exemption does not;
roles alone grant nothing under rulesets.

